\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Finite-group symmetry of Gaussian fields}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{The [half-turn](fdip-001A) splits fields into two sectors that its
commuting precision does not mix. Does symmetry still separate the field
when the order of exchanges matters? On the [uniform cycle](fdip-004W),
each complex scalar Fourier wave spans a one-dimensional sector labelled
by a discrete momentum. Real fields pair the nonexceptional momenta
#{k} and #{-k} in a two-dimensional sector. For a general finite group,
an isotypic sector collects all copies of one irreducible real
representation: precision preserves that whole sector but can mix
repeated copies within it. Orthogonal projectors recover these sectors
from complex characters paired under conjugation.
The prerequisites are [signed endpoint actions](fdip-003B),
[massive graph precision](fdip-000A) and the
[group-algebra extension](fdip-003N).}

\p{Signed symmetries preserve the quadratic energy even when an edge
is reversed. Maschke averaging and the complex central-idempotent
formula supply the representation theory; the real projector theorem
then gives a complete orthogonal decomposition preserved by precision.
The cyclic and dihedral calculations show the resulting subspaces and
eigenvalues explicitly.}

